  "register": "notes",
  "source_dataset": "MIMIC_IV_NOTE",
  "text": "Allergies:\nPenicillins\n\nChief Complaint:\nAbdominal Pain, Cardiac arrest\n\nHistory of Present Illness:\n___ y/o F w/PMHx recurrent nephrolithiasis, OSA, scoliosis and \nanxiety is transferred to ___ after what was felt to be a \ncardiac arrest. Patient initially presented to ___ \n___ on ___ with back pain and was found to have an \nobstructing 4.7mm Right ureteral stone. She was discharged with \nPercocet and a plan to f/u with urology. After discharge, pt was \nnot feeling well per her children. Of note, she was not using \nher home BiPAP, and she was only using her home 4L NC. Patient \nbecame increasingly lethargic and confused. On ___, patient \nreported to her son that she had a fall while trying to get out \nof a chair, and she hit her head. Her son called EMS and she was \nbrought to ___. There, she had a NCHCT that was \nnegative. While she was getting the CT scan, she was found to be \npale, apneic and pulseless, and chest compressions were started \nwith ROSC in ___ seconds. No medications or defibrillations \nwere necessary. Patient was intubated and required levophed \nshortly after intubation. She also received Narcan. She was \nsedated with propofol and transferred to ___ for further \nevaluation.\n\nIn the ED, initial vitals: 62; 141/77; 17; 100% RA. \n\nLabs were notable for:\n13.2>10.9/36.9<309 \nHCO3: 33\nCr: 0.5\nALT/AST: 33/51\nAP: 78 \nTbili: 0.7 \nAlb: 3.9 \nLip: 177\nTroponins: <0.01\nVBG:7.41/58/37/38\n \nPatient was given 500cc NS. \n \nOn arrival to the MICU,  VS were: 101.4; 90; 131/59; 21; 99% CMV \n35% at 350/17 w/PEEP 10.\n\nBrief Hospital Course:\n___ w/PMHx recurrent nephrolithiasis, OSA with chronic \nhypercarbia, scoliosis and anxiety, transferred to ___ from \n___ for possible cardiac arrest. \n\n# Question of Cardiac Arrest: Patient was hemodynamically stable \non arrival to the ___ MICU after transfer from OSH. Troponins \nwere negative and TTE was largely unremarkable. Bronchoscopy \nrevealed severe tracheobronchomalacia. Arrest, if it truly \noccurred, most likely was secondary to sudden hypoxemia in \nsetting of mucus plugging and her severe tracheobronchomalacia. \nVasovagal syncope and bradycardia were also considered, as was \nsevere hypophosphatemia which was corrected during her stay and \nseems to be due to malnutrition.\n\n#Pneumonia: On the day of admission, patient was started on \nciprofloxacin for a possible complicated UTI. She was febrile to \n101.4 on ___ with significant secretions, and was broadened to \nvancomycin/meropenem for presumed pneumonia. CT torso did not \nshow evidence of abscess. Bronchoscopy with BAL did not grow any \norganisms. Blood cultures were negative. Patient was narrowed to \nlevofloxacin on ___. On ___ (the day she was due to complete \ntreatment with levofloxacin), she became febrile to 100.7. She \nwas started on empiric vancomycin and cefepime for VAP coverage. \nMRSA swab was reported negative so vancomycin was discontinued. \nPlan is for an 8 day course of antibiotics (last day ___.\n\n#Respiratory failure: This was felt to be multifactorial, with \ncontributions from OSA, scoliosis, volume overload, \ntracheobronchomalacia (TBM) and pneumonia. On admission, there \nwas  difficulty weaning patient from the ventilator, and she \nrequired significant PEEP. Bronchoscopy on ___ showed \nright-sided erythema and friability, concerning for TBM. CTA on \n___ did not show e/o pulmonary embolism. Patient was \naggressively diuresed, with improvement in oxygen status. She \nwas extubated to BiPAP on ___. She continued to exhibit CO2 \nretention when off BiPAP for more than a few hours at a time. \nTherefore she will be discharged with a plan for at least 2 \nhours of BiPAP every 6 hours (even during the day) in addition \nto BiPAP at night and whenever she is napping during the day. \nShe will see interventional pulmonology in follow up as an \noutpatient.\n\n#Agitation: On admission to the ICU, patient was intubated and \nsedated on fentanyl/midazolam. She was transitioned to Precedex. \nOver the course of her hospitalization, she had continued \ndifficulty with agitation. This was treated intermittently with \nSeroquel with good effect. By day of discharge, however, patient \nwas no longer requiring Seroquel and she was NOT discharged on \nthis medication.\n\n#Nutrition: As above, patient was felt to be likely malnourished \ngiven low phosphate, magnesium and pre-albumin. She was started \non tube feeds through a Dobhoff tube. She may take a pureed diet \nwith nectar-thick liquids. \n\n#Nephrolithiasis: Patient was found at ___ \nto have an obstructing Right renal stone. Renal ultrasound did \nnot show evidence of hydronephrosis. Patient was discharged with \na plan to follow up with urology for further evaluation and \nmanagement.\n\n#Hypernatremia: Pt's sodium was 146 on day of discharge. She is \nreceiving frequent free water flushes through Dobhoff. She needs \na repeat sodium level on ___.\n\nTransitional Issues:\n-Follow up with interventional pulmonology for TBM\n-Follow up with urology for nephrolithiasis\n-Would recommend advancing nutrition as tolerated\n-Would recommend rechecking sodium on ___\n-BiPAP at night, during the day when napping, and for 2 hrs Q6H \nat all other times to help prevent CO2 accumulation (BiPAP \nSettings: ___\n-Please continue BOTH famotidine BID and PPI BID for treatment \nof tracheobronchomalacia\n-Please continue cefepime until ___ (8 day course) for \ntreatment of ventilator-associated pneumonia.\n\nDischarge Diagnosis:\nPrimary:\nRespiratory Failure with Pneumonia, Pulmonary Edema, and \nTracheobronchomalacia\nHistory of Obstructive Sleep Apnea and Hypoventilation Syndrome\n\nSecondary:\nNephrolithiasis",
